_3D.getMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
Returns the position on which a MU would be animated under certain marginal conditions.
Remarks
You can call _3D.getMUAnimationPosition for length-oriented objects and for point-oriented objects.
getMUAnimationPosition for Length-oriented Objects
Returns the anticipated position of a MU on the length-oriented object designated by <Path>.
If you call the method for a simulation object, the method evaluates the attribute _3D.AnimationObject
and, if need be, returns a position on an animated object within the coordinate system of the simulation
object.
If you call the method for the animatable object itself, the method only considers this object. Plant
Simulation assumes that the animation will take place on this animatable object and returns the position
within the coordinate system of the animatable object.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Path>._3D.getMUAnimationPosition(RelPos/AbsPos:real/length[, Lane/
Direction:integer:=1]) -> length[3]
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter RelPos/AbsPos is a value of data type real or length.
If the value has the data type real, it designates the relative position on the object. The relative position
can be a value between 0 and 1. 0 designates the start of the animation and 1 the end of the animation,
i.e., the value 100 %.
If the value has the data type length, it designates the absolute position on the object as the distance
starting at the beginning of the object.
• The optional parameter Lane/Direction of data type integer designates the lane or the direction on the
object respectively.
For a TwoLaneTrack the value 1 designates lane A and the value 2 designates lane B.
For a Converter the value 1 designates the default direction and the value 2 cross-wise conveying.
_3D.getMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
12-676 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

For all other objects 1 is the only valid value. If you do not specify the value, Plant Simulation
automatically uses the value 1.
Default Value of the Parameter
The default value is 1.
Return Value
The return value is an array of data type length with three values.
Example
var p := Converter._3D.getMUAnimationPosition(0.5, 2)
var p := Conveyor._3D.getMUAnimationPosition(1m)
getMUAnimationPosition for Point-oriented Objects
Returns the anticipated position of a MU on the point-oriented object designated by <Path>.
If you call the method for a simulation object, the method evaluates the attribute _3D.AnimationObject
and, if need be, returns a position on an animated object within the coordinate system of the simulation
object.
If you call the method for the animatable object itself, the method only considers this object. Plant
Simulation assumes that the animation will take place on this animatable object and returns the position
within the coordinate system of the animatable object.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Path>._3D.getMUAnimationPosition([X:integer:=1, Y:integer:=1]) -> length[3]
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The optional parameter X of data type integer designates the processing place in the X-direction.
You have to enter the parameter for objects with a matrix loading space.
For other objects the parameter is optional. If you do not specify the value, Plant Simulation
automatically uses the value 1.
For objects, which only have a single processing place, 1 is the only valid value.
_3D.getMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 12-677
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Default Value of the Parameter
The default value is 1.
• The optional parameter Y of data type integer designates the processing place in the Y-direction.
You have to enter the parameter for objects with a matrix loading space.
For other objects the parameter is optional. If you do not specify the value, Plant Simulation
automatically uses the value 1.
For objects, which only have a single processing place, 1 is the only valid value.
Default Value of the Parameter
The default value is 1.
Return Value
The return value is an array of data type length with three values.
Examples
var p := Station._3D.getMUAnimationPosition()
var p := PlaceBuffer._3D.getMUAnimationPosition(4)
var p := ParallelStation._3D.getMUAnimationPosition(3,4)
// Let's assume we have a container on the object Station.
// Then we can compute the position of the MU on place (1,1)
// in the current frame as follows:
var container := Station.Cont
var animPos   := Station.Cont._3D.getMUAnimationPosition(1,1)
print current._3D.getPositionOfObject(container, animPos)
